WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.161
This is the executive
briefing for Book 11 of the

2
00:00:03.161 --> 00:00:07.050
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Zero Trust and Assessment.

3
00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:12.147
It runs about thirty minutes, and it's
built for the people who make the calls: the

4
00:00:12.147 --> 00:00:17.400
CISO, the authorizing officials, the CIO,
and the Zero Trust implementation lead.

5
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.212
The book's argument fits
in three sentences.

6
00:00:20.212 --> 00:00:25.812
Zero Trust maturity is a property of the
whole agency, not of any product or tenant.

7
00:00:25.812 --> 00:00:29.951
The federal documents set specific
actions and configurations,

8
00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:34.474
but none of them defines a Zero Trust
score or requires a maturity stage.

9
00:00:34.474 --> 00:00:37.316
And the assessment tools
produce evidence for

10
00:00:37.316 --> 00:00:40.012
named people to act on,
not verdicts to report.

11
00:00:40.012 --> 00:00:44.534
I'll take four questions in order: why
Zero Trust belongs to the whole agency,

12
00:00:44.534 --> 00:00:48.763
what the mandates and the tools
actually require, who owns the posture,

13
00:00:48.763 --> 00:00:53.138
and how to turn findings into a program
the authorizing official can see.

14
00:00:53.138 --> 00:00:57.740
Every decision slide names the decision,
its owner, and when it's needed.
